(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 37-year-old woman has a firm nodule on the lower leg and reports that it occasionally hurts when knocked. What is notable about the symptom profile of a dermatofibroma?","choices":["Invariably painful","Always pruritic, never painful","Symptomatic only if over 3 cm","The commonest painful skin tumour"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dermatofibroma is usually asymptomatic, sometimes with a history of insect bite, and when symptomatic may cause slight pruritus or pain — it is described as the most common painful skin tumour. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who has lost 9 kg without trying is found to have new velvety hyperpigmented thickening of the axillae, neck, and, unusually, the palms and oral mucosa, which developed rapidly over three months. He is not obese and his fasting glucose is normal. What is the most appropriate concern?","choices":["Postinflammatory hyperpigmentation","Paraneoplastic acanthosis nigricans","Confluent and reticulated papillomatosis","Insulin-resistant acanthosis nigricans"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Malignant acanthosis nigricans is paraneoplastic, especially with gastrointestinal adenocarcinoma. Rapid onset, unusual sites including the palms and mucosa, and the absence of obesity or insulin resistance in an older patient with weight loss all point away from the common benign form. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 44-year-old runner has a painful lesion on the plantar surface of the forefoot. Pressing straight down on it reproduces the pain, whereas squeezing it from the sides does not. The skin lines run through the lesion, and paring it reveals a central cone of hard keratin. What is the diagnosis?","choices":["Verruca vulgaris","Callus","Epidermoid cyst","Clavus (corn)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A clavus results from pressure on a localised area, creating a cone-shaped central core of hard keratin. Pain occurs on direct downward pressure, and the skin lines run through the lesion. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 24-year-old wrestler has two itchy round plaques on the forearm. Examination shows sharply circumscribed slightly erythematous scaly rings with an advancing scaly border and a clearer centre. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pityriasis rosea","Tinea corporis","Nummular eczema","Granuloma annulare"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tinea corporis produces one or more circular, sharply circumscribed, slightly erythematous dry scaly plaques with an annular appearance and central clearing, acquired from contact with infected humans or animals. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man reports a single 4 cm oval salmon-coloured scaly plaque on his back ten days ago, followed by a shower of smaller similar lesions across the trunk whose long axes run along the rib lines. He feels well. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Secondary syphilis","Pityriasis rosea","Tinea corporis","Guttate psoriasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A herald patch precedes the exanthem in 50 to 90 percent of cases: a 2 to 5 cm oval salmon-coloured plaque that clears centrally, followed 1 to 2 weeks later by smaller lesions in the Christmas tree pattern. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy and his mother, who teaches at his school, both present with honey-crusted perioral lesions. In which patient with impetigo should a culture be obtained rather than relying on clinical appearance alone?","choices":["Any patient with honey-coloured crusting","A healthcare worker or teacher","Any patient on topical mupirocin","Any child under five"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cultures are obtained if the patient is at high risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus infection, with healthcare workers and teachers given as the examples, or if acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is a concern. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has scaly patches on the scalp with areas of hair loss and visible black dots where hairs have fractured. A potassium hydroxide preparation confirms fungal elements. His mother asks whether a medicated cream will be enough. What is the correct response?","choices":["An antifungal shampoo alone","No treatment; it resolves after puberty","Oral therapy; topicals miss the hair shaft","A topical cream to the lesion and beyond"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tinea capitis requires oral therapy; topical agents alone do not penetrate the infected hair shaft. Antifungal shampoo reduces viable spore shedding but does not replace oral treatment. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with obesity who smokes has recurrent painful nodules and draining lesions in both axillae and the groin, with five episodes in six months. Examination shows inflamed nodules and several sinus tracts. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acne vulgaris","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Furunculosis","Carbuncle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hidradenitis suppurativa is inflammation of apocrine glands affecting the axilla, groin, breasts, or perineum, and its three diagnostic elements are typical lesions, characteristic distribution, and recurrence more than twice in six months. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has an itchy papular eruption two days after swimming in a freshwater lake. What is the management of cercarial dermatitis?","choices":["Doxycycline for 5 to 10 days","Antihistamines and oatmeal baths","Extermination of the water source","Oral albendazole for 3 days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Management is symptomatic: antihistamines, oatmeal baths, antipruritic lotions, aspirin for pain control, proper washing and hygiene, and topical or oral glucocorticoids. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 24-year-old traveller returns from a rural area having walked barefoot on sandy soil. He has several erythematous papules on the feet that have enlarged over weeks into firm, somewhat translucent yellow nodules 4 to 10 mm across, some painful. What is the diagnosis and how is it confirmed?","choices":["Tungiasis, confirmed by dermoscopy visualising ovoid eggs","Cercarial dermatitis, confirmed by history of freshwater exposure","Scabies, confirmed by skin scraping","Cutaneous larva migrans, confirmed by the serpentine trail"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tungiasis is infestation by penetration of the adult female flea into human skin to lay eggs. Solitary or multiple erythematous papules enlarge over weeks to 4 to 10 mm, sometimes forming a firm translucent nodule, and dermoscopy is used to visualise the ovoid eggs. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man from Connecticut presents with a swollen, painful knee two years after an untreated tick bite. What is the classic manifestation of stage 3 late persistent Lyme disease?","choices":["Monoarticular arthritis of a large joint","Symmetric small-joint polyarthritis","Cranial nerve palsies and meningitis","A rash starting on ankles and wrists"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage 3 occurs months to years later, affecting skin, central nervous system, and musculoskeletal systems, with monoarticular or oligoarticular arthritis of the knee or weight-bearing joints as the classic manifestation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has confirmed toxic epidermal necrolysis with epidermal detachment of 45 percent of his body surface area. What is the mandatory disposition?","choices":["A burn unit or intensive care unit","A surgical ward for debridement","Emergency department observation","A general medical ward"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Burn unit or intensive care admission is mandatory for all cases of toxic epidermal necrolysis, together with immediate discontinuation of all suspect drugs, fluid resuscitation estimated as for major burns, and non-adhesive biological dressings. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A clinician seeing a 38-year-old woman with chest pain lists the diagnoses her initial cues suggest, then gathers further data to decide which are consistent with what has been observed. What does the hypothetico-deductive method involve?","choices":["Propose hypotheses, then test them against the data","Treat symptoms before a diagnosis is reached","Recognise patterns automatically","Rank diagnoses purely by frequency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The hypothetico-deductive method proposes hypotheses and tests whether they are acceptable or not by deciding whether the data are consistent with what has been observed. It runs from initial history, examination, and screening cues through hypothesis generation to hypothesis evaluation. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman presents with the sudden appearance of two smooth, round bald patches on her scalp, each about 3 cm across, that developed over three weeks. The underlying scalp is smooth without scaling, erythema, or scarring. Short hairs that are narrow at the root and normal in calibre at the tip are visible at the margins. She has a history of eczema and her mother has hypothyroidism. What finding is pathognomonic for the diagnosis?","choices":["A history of atopy","Round patches of loss","Exclamation point hairs","Sudden onset over three weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exclamation point hairs are pathognomonic for alopecia areata. Their growth ability is changing in real time, which produces a hair that is thinner at the root but normal in calibre at the top — exactly the description given here. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 138"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with obesity has a well-demarcated erythematous patch in the inframammary fold with several small papules and pustules on adjacent skin just beyond the main area of redness. What topical choice covers both possibilities?","choices":["Topical erythromycin","Topical terbinafine","Topical nystatin","A topical azole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Satellite papules and pustules accompany the well-demarcated erythematous patches of candidal intertrigo. Topical nystatin treats Candida only, whereas topical azoles treat both Candida and dermatophytes. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man in Connecticut has an expanding erythematous oval lesion 9 cm across with central clearing and a darker punctate centre at the site of a recent tick bite. He has mild fatigue. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cellulitis","Tinea corporis","Lyme disease","Rocky Mountain spotted fever"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stage 1 early localised Lyme disease is characterised by erythema migrans: a large expanding erythematous round or oval lesion over 5 cm with central clearing and often a darker punctate centre at the bite site, also called a bull's-eye. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman develops an itchy eruption of 2 to 4 mm erythematous papules across the upper chest and forearms a few hours after her first sunny weekend of spring. Her face, exposed year-round, is unaffected. She takes no medication. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Sunburn","Polymorphous light eruption","Phytophotodermatitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphous light eruption is the commonest idiopathic photodermatosis, appearing 30 minutes to hours after ultraviolet exposure in the first sunny days of spring, with papules on the décolletage and forearms and sparing of habitually exposed skin. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man in Tennessee has a painful lesion on the thigh two days after cleaning out a closet. Examination shows a central violaceous area surrounded by a rim of blanched skin, and beyond that a large area of erythema. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Black widow spider bite","Cellulitis","Hobo spider bite","Brown recluse spider bite"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The hallmark of a brown recluse bite is the red, white, and blue sign — a central violaceous area surrounded by a rim of blanched skin surrounded by a large erythematous area. Loxosceles reclusa is abundant in the American Midwest and Southeast and shelters in undisturbed places. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man develops an intensely pruritic vesicular eruption in linear streaks two days after clearing brush. What immunological mechanism underlies allergic contact dermatitis, and what is its most common cause in the United States?","choices":["Delayed type IV reaction, most often to nickel","Direct barrier disruption, no immune role","Immediate type I reaction to urushiol","Delayed type IV reaction, most often to urushiol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Allergic contact dermatitis is an immunologic cell-mediated, delayed, type IV hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol sap of Toxicodendron species, found in poison ivy, poison sumac, and poison oak, is the most common cause, and roughly 50 to 75 percent of people in the United States are allergic to these plants. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man is admitted with widespread painful erythema progressing to flaccid bullae and confluent epidermal detachment of about 40 percent of the body surface area, with a positive Nikolsky sign and severe mucosal erosions. What distinguishes this from Stevens-Johnson syndrome?","choices":["A prodrome","Mucosal involvement","A positive Nikolsky sign","Detachment over 30 percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The two conditions sit on one spectrum and are separated by the percentage of body surface area involved: Stevens-Johnson syndrome has less than 10 percent detachment, and toxic epidermal necrolysis has more than 30 percent, with mortality up to 30 to 35 percent. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has scaly annular plaques of the groin and scaling between the toes, and a student asks how these infections are named. How are dermatophyte infections classified, and what does the naming convey?","choices":["By species: Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton","By depth of dermal invasion","By the patient's age","By body location: capitis, corporis, cruris, pedis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dermatophyte infections are classified by body location: tinea capitis for scalp, barbae for beard, corporis for body, manuum for hand, cruris for the inguinal creases, and pedis for feet. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man in Oregon has a painless bite on the forearm that has become indurated with a large surrounding erythematous area. In which region of the United States is the hobo spider the predominant cause of necrotic arachnidism?","choices":["The Gulf Coast","The Northeast","The Midwest and Southeast","The Pacific Northwest"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The hobo spider, also called the aggressive house spider, is the predominant cause of necrotic arachnidism in the Pacific Northwest. The bite is painless, with induration and paraesthesia of the bite site within 30 minutes, a large erythematous area, and vesicle formation during the first 36 hours. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man presents with an itchy, scaling annular plaque on the trunk. The clinician wishes to confirm the presence of fungal elements at the bedside before starting treatment. What is the most appropriate test?","choices":["Tzanck smear","Nail clipping with periodic acid-Schiff stain","Potassium hydroxide wet preparation","Mineral oil preparation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A potassium hydroxide wet preparation detects fungal elements in skin, hair, or nail samples, and it is the appropriate bedside confirmation before treating a suspected dermatophyte infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman develops an itchy pustular eruption two days after using a hotel hot tub. Examination shows follicular papules and pustules over the trunk, buttocks, and proximal limbs, sparing the face, neck, palms, and soles. She is afebrile. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Shower after hot tub use to prevent it","Oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Reassurance; most resolve in 2 to 10 days","Oral ciprofloxacin for all patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pseudomonas folliculitis is usually self-limiting and most cases resolve without specific treatment, clearing in 2 to 10 days. A wet dressing of 5 percent acetic acid may be applied for 20 minutes 2 to 4 times daily for symptom relief. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a blotchy eruption on the trunk whose edges cannot be traced, and the clinician documents it as poorly demarcated. What does this term convey?","choices":["Smaller than 1 cm","Irregular, blotchy, poorly defined borders","Elevated above the surrounding skin","Clear borders but irregular internal colour"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A poorly demarcated lesion has an irregular or blotchy appearance without well-defined borders, in contrast to a well-demarcated lesion, which has clearly defined borders. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man reports three days of burning pain in a band across the left flank, followed by an eruption. Examination shows grouped vesicles on an erythematous base confined to a single band that stops abruptly at the midline. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bullous impetigo","Allergic contact dermatitis","Herpes simplex virus","Herpes zoster"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Herpes zoster is reactivation of latent varicella-zoster virus, preceded by dermatomal dysesthesia or pain, with grouped herpetiform vesicles on an erythematous base confined to one or two adjacent sensory nerves that stop abruptly at the midline. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has plantar warts that are not painful and do not interfere with walking. What is the appropriate management?","choices":["Cryotherapy every 2 to 3 weeks regardless of symptoms","Oral antiviral therapy to eradicate human papillomavirus","Daily salicylic acid 40 percent indefinitely to prevent recurrence","No therapy is required unless they are painful"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Plantar warts do not require therapy unless they are painful. They can cluster together to form a mosaic wart, and treatment when needed is salicylic acid 40 percent or cryotherapy. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 145"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl develops an abrupt eruption of numerous small raindrop-shaped scaly papules and plaques over the trunk and proximal limbs. Her mother reports she was treated for a sore throat about two and a half weeks ago. She is otherwise well. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Systemic corticosteroids tapered over four weeks","Reassurance, with topical steroids if needed","Topical mupirocin to the affected areas","Oral methotrexate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Guttate psoriasis presents as raindrop-shaped erythematous papules and plaques, typically in children 2 to 3 weeks following a streptococcal infection or upper respiratory infection. No treatment is needed, though phototherapy and topical steroids may be used. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 164"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with vitiligo affecting more than 5 percent of her body surface area is being counselled on treatment. What is first-line, and why is one phototherapy option preferred over another?","choices":["Surgical grafting for all patients","Psoralen with ultraviolet A preferred","Topical therapy alone at any extent","Phototherapy; narrow band ultraviolet B"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Phototherapy is first line in patients with more than 5 percent body surface area involvement, and narrow band ultraviolet B is preferred over psoralen with ultraviolet A, which carries adverse effects including increased skin cancer risk. Combination of topical therapy with phototherapy is ideal. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has a painful vesicular eruption in a band across one side of the chest. What is the single most characteristic distributional feature of herpes zoster?","choices":["It crosses the midline symmetrically","It is generalised over trunk and face","It stops abruptly at the midline","It is confined to skin folds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Blisters are confined to the cutaneous distribution of one or two adjacent sensory nerves and abruptly stop at the midline. Thoracic dermatomes are most frequently involved at about 55 percent, followed by cranial at about 20 percent. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has soft yellow plaques on the medial aspects of both upper eyelids. What do they contain?","choices":["Keratin within an epithelial cyst","Eccrine duct neoplasms","Lipid-laden macrophages","Dilated capillaries"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Xanthelasma consists of soft yellow cholesterol plaques formed by collections of lipid-laden macrophages. They are associated with lipid disorders, so screening for hyperlipidaemia is indicated, and they may signify increased cardiac risk. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy has several lesions around the nose and mouth that began as small blisters and now show golden-yellow adherent crusts over shallow erosions. There is palpable submandibular lymphadenopathy. He is afebrile. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ecthyma","Perioral dermatitis","Impetigo","Herpes simplex virus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-bullous impetigo is the commoner form: a macule evolves into a vesicle or pustule that ruptures, leaving honey-coloured adherent crust over an erosion, typically perioral in children, with regional lymphadenopathy common. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with progressive frontotemporal recession and early vertex thinning asks about treatment. He and his partner are not planning a pregnancy and he has no contraindications. Which statement about the two principal medical therapies is correct?","choices":["Minoxidil works best at the frontal hairline","Finasteride's sexual effects are permanent","Finasteride is topical and minoxidil oral","Finasteride is oral and works best with minoxidil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Finasteride prevents further hair loss and may provide some regrowth, and works best in combination with minoxidil. Dihydrotestosterone is the androgen chiefly responsible for the follicular miniaturisation, which is what the enzyme inhibition addresses. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome has red-purple plaques on the trunk and on the hard palate. What causes Kaposi sarcoma, and what does it arise from?","choices":["Human immunodeficiency virus, in endothelium","Ultraviolet damage to dermal fibroblasts","Human papillomavirus, in keratinocytes","Human herpesvirus 8, in vessel-lining cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Kaposi sarcoma develops in the cells lining blood and lymph vessels and is associated with human herpesvirus 8. It produces red or purple macules, plaques, or nodules on skin or mucous membranes and can involve internal organs. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 47-year-old fair-skinned woman reports facial flushing worsened by sun, hot drinks, and alcohol. Examination shows persistent central facial erythema with telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose. There are no comedones. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acne vulgaris","Acne rosacea","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Seborrheic dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The erythematotelangiectatic subtype produces persistent central facial erythema, flushing, and telangiectasias with sensitive stinging skin, triggered by sun exposure, heat, alcohol, and spicy food. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has a keratotic lesion on the ear that has enlarged over six weeks and now bleeds when he shaves. Which findings in a keratotic lesion are red flags for squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Rapid growth, pain, bleeding, and ulceration","A pearly translucent surface with telangiectasias","A stuck-on appearance with comedone-like openings","Sandpaper texture and small size"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Red flags for squamous cell carcinoma include rapid growth, pain, bleeding, and ulceration, and the same features in an actinic keratosis prompt biopsy to exclude early carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman is given silicone gel sheeting for a keloid on the chest and asks how it works and how long she must wear it. What is the theoretical basis for using it?","choices":["It delivers corticosteroid transdermally","It cools the scar, reducing blood flow","It warms the scar, raising collagenase activity","It induces tissue hypoxia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The theory is that occlusive dressings such as silicone gel sheets increase the temperature of the scar and thereby collagenase activity. They are worn 12 to 24 hours a day for up to a year. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has an itchy, sharply demarcated plaque on the proximal medial thigh extending from the crural fold. The scrotum is not involved. He also has scaling between his toes. What does the scrotal sparing indicate?","choices":["It supports tinea cruris","It indicates inverse psoriasis","It has no diagnostic significance","It indicates erythrasma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In tinea cruris the scrotum is typically spared, and scrotal involvement suggests candidal intertrigo, which commonly involves the scrotum. His coexisting tinea pedis fits, since the two often occur together. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman asks about treatment for photoaging. What topical agent is approved for this indication, and what should she be told to expect?","choices":["Tretinoin, with initial irritation over 6 to 12 months","Hydroquinone","Tretinoin, with results in two weeks and no irritation","Topical corticosteroids nightly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tretinoin 0.025 to 0.1 percent is the only approved topical agent for photoaging. It stimulates collagen synthesis, inhibits matrix metalloproteinase activity, and reduces fine lines and dyspigmentation; therapy is started low and titrated, with initial retinoid dermatitis expected. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man presents in late summer with numerous well-defined hypopigmented macules 4 to 5 mm over the upper back and shoulders, some coalescing. They did not tan with the rest of his skin. Scraping the surface reveals fine scale. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pityriasis versicolor","Pityriasis alba","Vitiligo","Postinflammatory hypopigmentation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pityriasis versicolor is caused by overgrowth of lipid-dependent Malassezia and produces velvety tan, pink, or white scaling macules 4 to 5 mm on the neck, upper arms, trunk, and groin. Hypopigmentation reflects altered melanocyte function and reduced tanning. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Four women in their twenties present on the same clinic list with acne of differing severity and history. For which patient is a combined oral contraceptive most appropriate as acne therapy?","choices":["A woman planning pregnancy soon","A man with papulopustular acne","Hyperandrogenism unresponsive to topicals","Any adolescent, as first-line therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oral contraceptives are considered for patients with hyperandrogenism and when acne is unresponsive to both topical retinoids and topical or oral antibiotics. Oestrogen decreases sebum production and reduces ovarian androgen production. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a moveable, round, firm nodule on the back with a visible central pore. It occasionally discharges foul-smelling material. It is not currently red or tender. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Abscess","Epidermoid cyst","Dermatofibroma","Lipoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An epidermoid cyst is a cystic enclosure of epithelium within the dermis filled with keratin, presenting as a single moveable round protruding mass with a central pore or punctum communicating with the surface. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman presents with a change in one fingernail and arrives wearing dark polish on every nail. What examination step is specified when evaluating a nail for a possible neoplasm?","choices":["Defer examination until pigment has been present for a year","Remove polish and inspect every nail","Perform a potassium hydroxide preparation before any inspection","Inspect only the symptomatic nail to avoid unnecessary examination"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. History and examination should document onset, evolution, trauma, bleeding, pain or cold sensitivity, prior treatments, medications, immune status, human papillomavirus risk, and personal or family melanoma history, and polish must be removed so that every nail can be inspected. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a soft, mobile, non-tender subcutaneous mass on the upper back that has enlarged very slowly over years. There is no overlying punctum, redness, or warmth. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Abscess","Dermatofibroma","Liposarcoma","Lipoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A lipoma is a benign localised overgrowth of fat cells in subcutaneous tissue and the most common soft tissue tumour, typically soft, mobile, and asymptomatic unless compressing a structure. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl starting treatment for acne of the face and back asks how quickly it will work. What education is accurate?","choices":["No improvement for at least six months","4 to 6 weeks, with all sites responding equally","4 to 6 weeks; back and chest take 3 to 4 months","Response cannot be measured objectively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Improvement in lesions can take 4 to 6 weeks, clinical improvement is measured by the number of new lesions after 6 to 8 weeks of therapy, and areas on the back and chest are slowest to respond, taking 3 to 4 months. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman presents on day four of a dermatomal vesicular eruption with new lesions still appearing. Within what window should antiviral therapy ideally be started, and when should it still be given beyond that window?","choices":["Within 72 hours only, never after","Only after polymerase chain reaction confirms it","Only once postherpetic neuralgia develops","Within 72 hours, and later if lesions still form"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Antivirals should be started as soon as possible, ideally within 72 hours of rash onset, and treatment is still given after 72 hours when new lesions are forming or there is ophthalmic involvement. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man taking allopurinol for four weeks has fever, stinging eyes, and painful swallowing, followed by painful erythema progressing to flaccid bullae and sheet-like skin loss over about 40 percent of his body surface. Lateral pressure on normal skin causes the epidermis to slide. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Toxic epidermal necrolysis","Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Pemphigus vulgaris","Erythema multiforme major"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Toxic epidermal necrolysis is drug-induced in over 80 percent of cases, with allopurinol the most common trigger worldwide. It produces confluent epidermal detachment over 30 percent of the body surface with a positive Nikolsky sign. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman presents with widespread itchy wheals that have been appearing and disappearing for three days. Individual lesions last a few hours, blanch fully, and leave no mark. There is no fever, no joint pain, and no bruising at resolved sites. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Intramuscular epinephrine","Skin biopsy before treatment","A second-generation antihistamine","A first-generation antihistamine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Second-generation antihistamines are first-line for urticaria. Urticaria results from mast cell degranulation releasing histamine, prostaglandins, and leukotrienes, causing transient dermal oedema. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has a suspicious pigmented growth on the back that the clinician wishes to sample in a way that removes the entire lesion with deep and wide margins. What biopsy technique is appropriate?","choices":["Curettage of the lesion surface","Punch biopsy","Fine needle aspiration of the lesion","Excisional biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An excisional biopsy removes the whole lesion taking all deep and wide margins, and always needs stitches to close the wound. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man returns from a hotel stay with several itchy welts on exposed skin. Examination shows wheals and papules with a haemorrhagic punctum arranged in rows of three. He did not feel the bites occur. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Scabies","Bedbugs","Flea bites","Cercarial dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bedbugs are nocturnal feeders hiding in cracks and crevices of headboards and furniture. Bites are painless, multiple, and grouped in a linear fashion, with a row of three known as breakfast, lunch, and dinner, and reactions may be wheals and papules with a haemorrhagic punctum. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man is found to have hundreds of small papules over the trunk and extremities consistent with generalised granuloma annulare. What screening is most appropriate?","choices":["Fasting glucose and haemoglobin A1c","Chest radiograph","No screening is indicated","Immunoglobulin A tissue transglutaminase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fasting glucose and haemoglobin A1c are used to screen for diabetes in generalised granuloma annulare, alongside a lipid panel and thyroid studies for associated systemic disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with numerous naevi asks how she should check her own moles between clinic visits. What do the letters of the ABCDE recognition rule stand for?","choices":["Asymmetry, border, colour, diameter, evolution","Age, burn history, complexion, dysplasia, exposure","Asymmetry, border, colour, dermoscopy, excision","Asymmetry, bleeding, crusting, depth, erosion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asymmetry means one half does not match the other; border refers to irregular, notched, or poorly defined edges; colour describes variegation with brown, red, white, black, or blue within one lesion; diameter and evolution complete the rule. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has a painful fluctuant nodule 4 mm across on the back of the neck. He is afebrile and it is a single lesion. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Immediate incision and drainage","Warm compresses plus oral cephalexin","Oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Warm compresses without antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Warm compresses are usually sufficient for small furuncles, and no antibiotics are given if the patient is afebrile with a single lesion under 5 mm. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has several tense, clear-to-cloudy blisters on the trunk arising on intact skin. Some have ruptured, leaving shallow moist erosions ringed by a collarette of scale. There is no lymphadenopathy. What organism and mechanism are responsible?","choices":["Staphylococcus or Streptococcus, via a skin break","Autoantibody against the basement membrane","Staphylococcus aureus alone, via epidermolytic toxin","Streptococcus pyogenes, by dermal invasion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bullous impetigo is caused exclusively by Staphylococcus aureus, which releases epidermolytic toxins producing epidermal splitting. Bullae occur on intact skin, rupture to leave collarettes, and lymphadenopathy is uncommon — in contrast to non-bullous disease, where it is common. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 5-month-old girl has a large hemangioma of the face lying close to the eye and the corner of the mouth. What complications should be considered?","choices":["No complications occur","Systemic hypertension from shunting","Malignant transformation to angiosarcoma","Compression of vital structures"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemangiomas may compress vital structures, block vision, interfere with feeding or respiration, obstruct the external auditory canal, and occur at extracutaneous sites. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A student is seeing a 46-year-old woman with an undifferentiated complaint and is unsure how to begin working through it. What are the first three steps of the clinical reasoning process?","choices":["Generate hypotheses, gather, then test","Apply a guideline, then confirm it fits","Treat symptoms, observe, then diagnose","Gather, interpret, then represent the problem"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The process begins with gathering initial patient information, then organising and interpreting it, then synthesising the information into a problem representation, before hypotheses are generated and tested. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 50-year-old labourer has a thickened, dry, scaly right palm that he has attributed to hard physical work. He also has scaling on both soles. What is the likely diagnosis, and how does it typically present?","choices":["Tinea manuum","Irritant contact dermatitis","Dyshidrotic eczema","Chronic paronychia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tinea manuum is associated with tinea pedis, and both palms and soles can be infected at the same time. Patients are often unaware of the infection, believing the changes are due to dry skin or hard physical labour, and the involved palm is thickened, dry, and scaly. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man presents six hours after a day at the beach with painful erythema, warmth, and tenderness across the shoulders and back. There is no blistering. What is the most appropriate acute management?","choices":["Immediate systemic corticosteroids","Cool compresses, early anti-inflammatories, hydration","Cold immersion and delayed anti-inflammatories","Prophylactic oral antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute management is cool compresses and cool rather than cold water immersion, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs started early to reduce prostaglandin-mediated inflammation and pain, oral hydration, and topical moisturisers. First-degree sunburn resolves in 3 to 5 days with desquamation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man presents with an engorged tick attached to his flank after a hike in an endemic area. What single action is emphasised first in the management of a tick-borne illness such as Lyme disease?","choices":["Await the appearance of erythema migrans before acting","Administer prophylactic intravenous antibiotics to all patients","Send the tick for species identification before any treatment","Remove the tick immediately"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The management sequence begins with removing the tick immediately, followed by antibiotics, which are indicated in all stages. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has been applying a topical corticosteroid to the same area of eyelid skin for several months. What adverse effects should be anticipated?","choices":["Hyperpigmentation, hypertrichosis, and thickening of the dermis","Permanent loss of hair follicles at the treated site","Photosensitivity, dryness, and irritation","Atrophy, striae, telangiectasia, and hypopigmentation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prolonged use of topical corticosteroids may cause atrophy, striae, telangiectasia, and hypopigmentation. This is why potency is selected according to site and severity and why the usual course is twice daily for two weeks. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a firm 8 mm nodule on her lower leg with a brown halo and a slightly raised centre. When the skin on either side is squeezed together, the lesion dimples downward. What is this sign, and what does dermoscopy typically show?","choices":["Nikolsky sign","The dimple sign, seen in lipoma","Darier sign","The dimple sign"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The dimple sign is characteristic of dermatofibroma, which is a firm 0.5 to 1 cm nodule formed by dense clusters of dermal fibroblasts, most commonly on the legs. Dermoscopy often shows a peripheral pigment network with a central white area. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy has a circumscribed light brown patch on the trunk resembling a café au lait spot, within which several darker pigmented macules and papules are visible. What is this lesion?","choices":["A dysplastic naevus","A blue nevus","Nevus spilus","A cafe au lait macule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nevus spilus, the spotted nevus, is a variant of congenital naevus present at birth or in the first years of life, most commonly on the trunk and extremities. Darkly pigmented macules or papules sit within a background that is circumscribed and similar in appearance to a café au lait spot, and it rarely progresses to melanoma. Management is observation, periodic evaluation, and sun protection. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man is stung by a wasp and within minutes develops widespread urticaria, wheezing, and hypotension. What is the immediate treatment?","choices":["Cleaning, ice, and local anaesthetic","An oral antihistamine at home","Local wound debridement","Intramuscular or subcutaneous epinephrine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For an anaphylactic reaction to a hymenoptera sting the treatment is subcutaneous or intramuscular epinephrine with emergency department transfer and supportive measures. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with diabetes has an asymptomatic brownish patch in a skin fold noted on routine examination. Where is erythrasma most commonly found, and what is the typical symptom profile?","choices":["Scalp, thighs, and trunk, with follicular pustules","Face and lower leg, with sudden high fever","Axilla, groin, and perineum, recurrent and painful","Inner thighs, groin, and fourth toe web; often silent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The most common sites are the inner thighs, crural region, scrotum, and between the fourth and fifth toes, with the axilla, under the breasts, and intergluteal folds less common. It is usually asymptomatic and may be pruritic. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man presents with a painful swelling in the natal cleft, and the clinician reviews what predisposed him to it. Which set of risk factors is associated with pilonidal disease?","choices":["Prematurity and low birth weight","Venous insufficiency and lymphoedema","Female sex, pregnancy, oestrogen excess","Obesity, sedentary life, dense cleft hair"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those are the listed risk factors, and the male to female ratio is 3 to 1. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 39"}]};
